From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: show GL frames as IOSurfaces

With gl=on the window's CAOpenGLLayer drew the guest's scanout on the
main thread, inside drawInCGLContext, holding the BQL: every frame the
main thread waited for QEMU's lock and QEMU waited for the main thread.

Draw the scanout into one of three IOSurfaces instead, on QEMU's thread
with a context of its own, and show that surface as the layer's contents.
A serial queue waits for the GPU to finish a surface before Core Animation
gets it; a surface is reused only once Core Animation no longer uses it
(IOSurfaceIsInUse). The main thread no longer takes the BQL to draw.
Core Animation composites the surface directly. At most one surface is
drawn per display refresh (a timer catches up with the newest frame), so a
guest that flushes thousands of times a second costs no more than that.

OMACVM_GL_PRESENT=layer goes back to the CAOpenGLLayer path; if the
IOSurface contexts cannot be made, the window falls back to it as well.
OMACVM_GL_DUMP=FILE writes a frame shown as a PPM whenever FILE is missing
(checks orientation and colours). Applies after qemu-cocoa-gl-present-on-flush.

